Summary
As Zambia transitions from an agriculture-based economy to a technology-driven one, the demand for locally trained technologists has increased. In response to this shift, Shimbo Academy is adapting its curriculum to align with the changing job market by introducing technology-focused classes. Additionally, the Academy plans to integrate technology into classrooms to enhance ongoing education for students and teachers.
To realign the curriculum, Shimbo Academy needed to establish a new network that had not existed before. This network is required to connect to the Internet, support 150 end-user devices, ensure security, segment traffic, and maintain reliable connectivity.
POS-Networking was awarded the contract to design, implement, and support the network according to the Academy's specifications. The solution utilized Starlink as the Internet Service Provider (ISP) and Omada-based Software Defined Networking (SDN) technology for routing and switching. The network was segmented into five VLANs to enhance security and efficiency, ensuring smooth traffic flow.
During the project, POS-Networking encountered several unforeseen challenges. The first hurdle was a delivery delay involving the Omada ER7412-M2 Gateway and the Omada EAP211 Bridge, which were initially reported as available but were back-ordered. Additionally, there was a delay at Zambia Customs due to a paperwork error, as the required import tax document was not attached. We had to request import tax documentation from the vendor to prove that the necessary duties were paid. As a result, the installation of network equipment and cabling was delayed by about one month.
Another issue we faced was the customer's request for POS-Networking to maintain the network after installation. We addressed this ongoing maintenance concern by implementing Omada SDN technology, which allows us to monitor, update, and maintain the network remotely. This technology enabled us to offer maintenance at a lower cost compared to having a technician on-site. Additionally, it allowed us to utilize real-time data while teaching networking courses to students.
The project concluded on August 22, 2024, after a week-long evaluation to ensure the network met our standards of an average upload speed of 100 Mbps and a download speed of 25 Mbps across the local area network (LAN) and through the ISP provider, with bandwidth utilization remaining below 80% of the 1 Gbps LAN capacity.
Shimbo Academy accepted the project on August 24, 2024, expressing satisfaction with our work. They recognized that the project fulfilled their initial goals of introducing external learning resources into classrooms, offering technology classes to students, and providing ongoing education for teachers. The Academy also saw the potential to expand its offerings to deliver these courses to more students while increasing its financial revenue.
	


Review of Other Work
Review of Work 1 
During the network implementation, we encountered a performance issue that negatively impacted the network's speed. Reviewing the logs, we discovered that our traffic was subjected to double NAT: first by the Starlink router and then again by our Omada Gateway. (Starlink, n.d., para. 1).
Starlink recommends placing the Starlink router into "Bypass" mode to resolve this issue. Activating the "Bypass" mode allowed the Omada router to function as the primary router, with the Starlink router serving solely as a WAN connection. This configuration streamlined traffic flow and eliminates the complications caused by double NAT. (Starlink, n.d., para. 1).

Review of Work 2
The Omada Software Defined Network (SDN) Cloud Controller's "Zero Touch" functionality simplifies the setup of network components and reduces the chance of configuration errors (TP-Link Corporation Limited, 2022). This controller automatically assigns devices to the correct VLANs and configures all necessary settings, such as SID, VLAN, routes, and port security.

This functionality allowed us to save valuable person-hours in device configuration. The time saved was redirected to the critical CAT-5e installation, which was at risk of being delayed.	 

Review of Work 3 
Before my initial discussions with Shimbo Academy, I recalled Kishor Singh's article, "Harnessing Digital Technologies for Education in Developing Countries: Need for a Judicious Approach," which highlights important considerations for technologists looking to support underprivileged regions.
One of the key points Singh raises is the necessity for caution when expanding technology in education. Although developing countries may view the internet as a quick route to progress, there are often overlooked trade-offs—such as the potential for increased isolation, a phenomenon that has been commonly observed in developed nations. Moreover, the cultural shifts induced by technology can be significant, akin to those experienced in the United States between Generation X and Millennials (Singh, 2023, sect. "Need for a Cautious and Critical Approach").
Another concern highlighted by Singh is the rapid privatization of education as it shifts from traditional classrooms to digital platforms. In some developing countries, policymakers face pressure from large corporations to continue this trend, which further exacerbates the educational gap between developed and developing nations (Singh, 2023, sect. "Safeguarding Education from Forces of Privatization, Fortified by Digitalization in Education" & "Overcoming the 'Digital Divide'").
As a technologist who has engaged with Singh's insights, I found them valuable for discussing the potential pitfalls and limitations that the network project could impose on students and teachers. His observations helped me recommend that the administration implement stricter content controls on social media and advocate with the government for free education for all. 

Changes to the Project Environment
At the beginning of the project, there was no network infrastructure, meaning staff managed student records using paper files. Teachers conducted their lessons on chalkboards and distributed handouts to students. The school relied on the "Sneaker Net" method to transfer documents between the few standalone systems that were operational. This setup did not align with the client's goal of preparing students for a technology-driven future.
Deploying the network on campus dramatically transformed the environment. The administration is now able to store student files on a server, improving efficiency and accessibility. Teachers can incorporate a wider variety of learning resources directly into the classroom. Additionally, the school has introduced technology courses such as CompTIA A+, CompTIA Net+, CompTIA Sec+, and Cisco CCNA, providing students with valuable skills for the future.
One challenge that arose during the project was the need for stricter content monitoring on the firewall. The initial configuration allowed overly broad access, leading to scope deviation in classroom instruction. To resolve this issue, we collaborated with the school during the initial phase to develop a whitelist for the “SC-Teacher” VLAN and implement content restrictions.
Overall, the completed project met Shimbo Academy's expectations. The network brought significant positive changes to the school environment, setting students on a path toward a technology-focused future.
Methodology
For the Shimbo Academy Network Project, I utilized the Waterfall methodology, which consisted of four distinct phases: Planning, Design, Implementation, and Testing. This method was selected because each phase depended heavily on the completion and success of the previous one, making a linear project flow ideal.
During the Planning phase, we held multiple meetings with school administrators to refine the scope of the project. The initial meeting focused on reviewing the “Request for Bid,” which outlined the need to design a network from the ground up to support at least 150 users with internet access—an essential component of the school’s shift toward technology-based education. This meeting also served as an introduction to our team, during which we shared a conceptual network design and a budget proposal to align with the school's goals.
A key point of discussion was that POS-Networking would  be responsible for the physical buildout of the network infrastructure and would also maintain the network and act as adjunct instructors for the school's networking courses. While POS-Networking initially viewed this as scope creep, we proposed a solution to split the work into two distinct projects—“Building” and “Maintaining” the network. Both parties agreed to proceed on these terms, and a follow-up meeting was scheduled to finalize the design.
At the second project meeting, we presented the proposed timeline for review and client approval. We also walked through the Network Physical Diagram (Appendix A) and the Network Logical Diagram (Appendix B). During this session, we were informed that the school had recently acquired two servers that needed to be integrated into the design. Our lead architect made the necessary adjustments on the spot to avoid delays, and the final design was approved. At the conclusion of the meeting, all contracts were signed, allowing us to move forward to the next project phase.
    
