	The Implementation Phase of the project—which encompassed procurement, configuration, and installation—was where we encountered project delays. Initially, we estimated that procuring the STARLINK system, OMADA gateway, switches, wireless bridge, access points, CAT-5e cabling, and uninterruptible power supplies (UPS) would take approximately five weeks from purchase to site delivery. However, due to unforeseen delays in logistics, it ultimately took eight weeks for all components to arrive on-site for configuration.
The Configuration sub-phase was completed one week ahead of schedule. This efficiency was largely due to the implementation of the OMADA Software Defined Networking (SDN) solution, which allowed the network to be dynamically configured as devices came online.
During the Installation sub-phase, POS-Networking encountered a challenge running CAT-5e cable through the facility. The building’s cinder block construction made it difficult to secure cable mounts properly. Resolving this issue added an extra week to the schedule. Fortunately, we mitigated a potential two-week delay by reallocating manpower—reassigning engineers to assist technicians and accelerate installation.     
During network stress testing, engineers observed that bandwidth loss did not exceed 5% (Appendix C). Efficient bandwidth utilization was achieved by configuring the OMADA gateway to restrict content that typically causes network congestion, such as streaming services and social media platforms.

