	During the procurement phase for the Cat-5e ceiling run, we encountered a delay when attempting to source traditional wire racks. The required cable trays had a 12-week lead time, which was deemed unacceptable for the project timeline. To maintain progress, we locally sourced aluminum rain gutters as an alternative. These effectively supported and elevated the wire runs throughout the building, achieving the same outcome as conventional trays.
The second issue arose during the NAT configuration of the internal ports to the STARLINK IP address. Initially, the network operated as expected, with internal IPs successfully NATed to the external IP. However, during the testing phase, we observed that NAT functionality had ceased. Upon investigation, we determined that STARLINK was assigning IPs via DHCP, resulting in an unexpected IP address change. This raised security concerns, as our team was committed to maintaining a NATed environment. After reviewing STARLINK’s documentation and consulting with OMADA support, we reconfigured the OMADA device’s two WAN ports to enable NAT with DHCP-assigned IPs.
Despite these two challenges, POS-Networking successfully delivered all promised outcomes to Shimbo Academy. As with any IT project, we anticipated unforeseen issues and had built adequate buffer time into the project schedule to accommodate them.

