Unanticipated Scope Creep
The initial Request for Bid from Shimbo Academy focused on designing and installing a network to support their goal of integrating technology into the classroom. During the initial meeting with the school's leadership, the leadership proposed that taking on the contract would also require POS-Networking to maintain the network. 
The first scope creep occurred when we adjusted the network design to accommodate long-term maintenance. We added the Omada Software Defined Networking (SDN) cloud controller to the design and implementation to address this additional requirement. The Omada SDN allowed us to perform remote monitoring, maintenance, and security functions. However, including this SDN package increased the initial bid cost, as we needed to factor in the price of the subscription and the personnel required for network maintenance.
The second alteration to the original plan happened when the Academy acquired two servers designated for teacher and staff use, which would not have access to the Internet. The new requirement necessitated the creation of a subnet to accommodate the new infrastructure. Consequently, we modified our initial design by adding the 192.8.25.0/31 subnet to connect the two servers to the LAN. We allowed access only to the SC-Teacher (Teacher) and SC-Admin (Administration) VLANs. At the same time, all other routing policies explicitly denied routing traffic to the 192.8.25.0/31 subnet. 
The scope creep resulted from the Academy's lack of communication regarding their complete requirements in the original Request for Bid documents. Despite these changes, POS-Network accepted the requests because they aligned with the Academy's valuable mission. The adjustments were managed effectively, ensuring the project's success.
Conclusion
At the end of the testing period, we evaluated the project's success by confirming that each device could access internet resources such as YouTube and the Zambia Ministry of Education's website from both the wireless local area network (WLAN) and the ports on the local area network (LAN). Additionally, we were able to navigate from a test laptop on the SC-Teacher VLAN to the storage server, demonstrating that we had established internal connectivity on the LAN.
The Wide Area Network (WAN) connection maintained an average upload speed of 169 Mbps and a download speed of 57 Mbps (see Appendix C). This was measured over seven days across both the LAN and WAN. The connection speed was significantly higher than our initial estimates of 100 Mbps for uploads and 25 Mbps for downloads. We believe this discrepancy was due to our initial estimates being based on Starlink's personal plan rather than their business plan, which we ultimately selected.
During testing, we deployed five teams simultaneously throughout the school to access the internet and LAN resources in order to measure bandwidth utilization. We observed that bandwidth utilization never exceeded 30% (see Appendix C), which met our standard of keeping utilization below 80%. However, this test may be a false positive indicator, as POS-Networking could not fully simulate the entire network load. If bandwidth issues arise as production continues, we can address them by managing traffic more effectively and adding a Starlink system to separate the VLANs between the two Starlink systems.
The immediate impact of our project became apparent when teachers began to incorporate additional resources, such as YouTube, Google Search, and CB-Nugget courses, into their classrooms. These internet resources enabled students to visualize the concepts they were learning rather than just imagining them. Furthermore, staff and teachers could efficiently communicate student grades and provide specialized assistance to those requiring extra support using the two servers.
However, not all immediate outcomes were entirely positive. Some students attempted to misuse the network, which can be common behavior for both children and adults. In response, POS-Networking collaborated with school administrators to tighten the whitelist, striking a balance between accessibility and security.
Overall, the project serves as a model for other stakeholders in Zambia, including the Ministry of Education, demonstrating how schools can lead technological advancements. For many small, rural schools in Zambia, which often lack direct access to circuits, a low-earth orbit (LEO) connection combined with a small network can provide students with opportunities to embrace the future and reach their full potential.


